From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 09:21:50 +0000
Subject: net: ethernet: lantiq: bring up all four GRX350 LAN egress ports

eth0..eth3 all register at probe, but only eth0 could ever be brought
up: "ip link set eth1 up" fails -EINVAL in ndo_open step 1. Everything
below the netdev was single-port, and three separate places had to learn
about the other three.

hdma_port_enable() knew CBM dequeue ports 7 and 10 only, so eth1 (deq 9)
and eth2 (deq 8) fell straight into its -EINVAL arm. Add
port8_channels/port9_channels for DMA2TX channels 3 and 4, and factor
the enable/disable dispatch into one hdma_port_chan_tbl() lookup so the
two can never disagree about which channels belong to a port again: they
did once, disable knowing only port 7, which leaked eth0's channel 5
across an ndo_stop/ndo_open cycle.

eth3 (deq 7) matched, but onto the wrong table. The port-7 entry carried
.cbm_deq = 0, left from when port 7 was a bring-up test channel rather
than eth3's egress, and .cbm_deq is a mode selector, not decoration:
zero routes the channel into the legacy software-ring path, which
allocates a DRAM descriptor ring the CBM never writes to and which the
DMA therefore reads while the CBM dequeue engine writes elsewhere. All
four of these ports are CBM-managed -- their descriptors live in the CBM
DQM window at 0x1e540000 + (deq - 5) * 0x1000 -- so all four entries
must set .cbm_deq non-zero. Set the port-7 entry to 7, and say so in the
table's comment.

The DQM descriptor-window formula is established for deq 7 and deq 10,
so 8 and 9 interpolate between two known points rather than
extrapolating past them.

The scalar g_hdma_port10_armed latch becomes a bitmap over deq 7..10.
Its semantics are unchanged -- arm once, keep armed for the device
lifetime, because closing and reopening a CBM-managed channel leaves the
DQM-to-DMA2TX handshake stale -- and now apply per port.
hdma_port_disable() still refuses to tear down an armed channel, but an
in-range port that is not armed now falls through to the teardown: that
is the ndo_open rollback path for an enable that failed partway, where
releasing what was acquired is correct.

cbm_dp_port_alloc()'s dma_chan switch resolved only dp 5 and dp 4 and
sent dp 2 and dp 3 to a DMA_CHANNEL_0 placeholder. Replace the whole
switch with the closed form: dp N -> DMA2TX channel N, because dequeue
port N+5 is served by DMA2TX channel (deq - 5) == N. The placeholder's
comment was doubly stale -- it cited a superseded "+2" map and claimed
hdma_port_enable() rejects any port_id != 7, which had not been true
since port 10 was armed.

Third, the probe-time egress preconfiguration ran for dp5 only. dp 2/3/4
had no probe-time DQM/TMU egress path, so they fell through to the
cbm_dp_enable() path at ndo_open, i.e. after cbm_enable_controllers() --
the configure-after-enable inversion that was already fixed for dp5,
where a DQM enabled before its EPMAP binding rebuilds the egress
descriptor from a stale PPT body length and reports data_len = 0. Loop
the preconfiguration over dp 2..5, deriving deq = dp + 5, qid = dp + 15
and sbid = qid - SBID_START, and loop the probe-tail hdma_port_enable()
over deq 7..10 as well. This is what the vendor driver does: its
configure_ports() walks all 17 compiled-in rows at probe, so every DMA
egress port is bound before the controllers come up. The loop stays
where the single-port version was, before cbm_enable_controllers(); that
ordering is load-bearing and the code now says so.

Finally, make the GSW-L whole-core re-init a one-shot at its own entry
point. gsw_hw_reinit_gswl() pulses ETHSW_SWRES, which takes every port
of the instance down, reloads the PCE microcode and wipes the switch TOP
block including the MDIO master. That is acceptable exactly once, while
at most one port is passing traffic; it is not acceptable once per
netdev, where a later port's ndo_open would drop the links of ports
already forwarding. Its single caller is structurally one-shot already
-- one delayed work scheduled from the MDIO driver's probe -- but that
is a property of the caller, not of the function. The latch makes it a
property of the function, so a future caller cannot turn a deliberate
one-shot into a per-port switch reset.

No new CBM, DQM, EQM or TMU resource is allocated by any of this. All 17
DQM DMA egress ports, their DMA TX channels and their TMU triplets are
statically pre-provisioned and disjoint per port; the four LAN ports
differ only in which of them each call names. Nothing else needed
touching: the GSWIP-L per-port FDMA/SDMA/BM enables, the MDIO auto-poll
mask (0x003C = ports 2..5), MAC_FLEN, the PMAC ingress/egress tables
(indexed by TX DMA channel, so channels 2..5 map to ports 2..5 for
free), the GPHY firmware loader and phylink are all four-port already.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

